test(reconciler): give RM-104's cases their catalog ids, and report the drift (#169)

RM-104's check shipped with eight tests and no doc 07 rows, so its cases had no
catalog identity. Assigning them found the hazard the issue itself warned about:
"nothing in this repository enforces test-ID uniqueness, and three collisions
have already been caught by hand this week."

Three more were caught by hand here. REC-009, REC-010 and REC-011 were about to
go to these cases while the rebase tests already held them — and the reason that
was invisible is that those three had no catalog row to collide with. The writes
cases are REC-006..008 and REC-012..016; the rebase cases keep the ids they
already use and now have rows describing them.

scripts/test-ids.sh reports both directions, and it found more than it was
written for: 75 test ids exist in the code with no row in doc 07, across GH,
LED, MCP, OPS, SEG, SER, SPI and VER. Every one of them is an id the next person
can reassign with nothing to notice the collision. Cataloguing them is the
maintainer's call and is not done here — doc 07 is a spec document, and 75 rows
invented by inference is exactly what "never edit a spec document to match an
inference" forbids.

It is a `make` target and not a CI gate, because doc 07 is local only: `docs/*`
is gitignored, so a runner has no catalog to check against. Saying that in the
script is better than a gate that silently passes because it found no catalog.

Two measurements shaped it. The reverse direction cannot look only for Go
functions — shell self-tests carry their ids in comments and the frontend's
cases are TypeScript, so a `func Test` search reported thirty FE rows as
untested when every one is a .test.tsx. And its default catalog path resolves
against the REPOSITORY, because docs/* is gitignored and a worktree does not
carry it: the same shape as RM-148's link and RM-150's log pin, found by running
it from a worktree and getting "no catalog".

What it cannot see is stated in the script: two different cases sharing one
catalogued id read here as one id with several tests, which is also the normal
and correct shape — REC-004 has five test functions, all facets of one case.
Telling those apart needs someone who knows what the case is about. What this
removes is the silent half.

